  "ssml": "<speak version=\"1.0\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis\" xmlns:mstts=\"https://www.w3.org/2001/mstts\" xml:lang=\"en-PH\">\n  <voice name=\"en-PH-JamesNeural\">\n    <mstts:express-as style=\"friendly\">\n      <prosody rate=\"96%\" pitch=\"+2%\">\n        Alright, <break time=\"300ms\"/> Kuya James here. <break time=\"550ms\"/> Whenever an <phoneme alphabet=\"ipa\" ph=\"əˈnælədʒi\">analogy</phoneme> looks <emphasis level=\"moderate\">tricky</emphasis>, <break time=\"300ms\"/> don&apos;t rush. <break time=\"550ms\"/> Try this <emphasis level=\"moderate\">three-step</emphasis> <emphasis level=\"moderate\">bridge</emphasis> trick. <break time=\"550ms\"/> First, <break time=\"300ms\"/> cover the choices so they don&apos;t distract you. <break time=\"550ms\"/> Second, <break time=\"300ms\"/> make a short sentence for the <emphasis level=\"moderate\">first pair</emphasis>, <break time=\"300ms\"/> like: a <emphasis level=\"moderate\">plank</emphasis> is a part of a <emphasis level=\"moderate\">bridge</emphasis>. <break time=\"550ms\"/> Third, <break time=\"300ms\"/> plug the <emphasis level=\"moderate\">second pair</emphasis> into that exact same sentence!\n      </prosody>\n    </mstts:express-as>\n  </voice>\n</speak>",
